# Sınav sonuç ekranı için ek örnek cevaplar: yazma görevlerine 2 ek örnek, konuşma sorularına süreyi dolduran 2 örnek
W={
'a2x-w1':[('Örnek 2 · farklı fikirlerle','Hi Alex,\nThe weather is going to be sunny on Saturday, so shall we go to Central Park? We can meet at the café near the big gate at 2 o\'clock. I\'ll bring my bike, so we can ride around the lake. After that we can have an ice cream.\nBye,\nZeynep'),
 ('Örnek 3 · daha zengin dil (A2 üstü)','Hi Alex,\nAre you free on Saturday? I\'ve got a great idea – let\'s go to the park by the river! I think we should meet at half past ten at the bus stop opposite my school, because it\'s easy for both of us. There\'s a new skate park there, so we can try it, and if we get hungry, we can have a picnic under the trees.\nSee you,\nBerk')],
'a2x-w2':[('Örnek 2 · farklı fikirlerle','On Sunday afternoon, Tom was playing football with his friends in the park. He kicked the ball very hard, but it went over the trees and hit a window. The glass broke! Tom was very scared. He went to the house and said sorry to the old woman. She wasn\'t angry. She smiled and gave him a cookie.'),
 ('Örnek 3 · daha zengin dil (A2 üstü)','Last summer, Emir was practising football in the park next to his grandmother\'s street. He wanted to score a great goal, so he kicked the ball as hard as he could. Unfortunately, it flew into a garden and smashed a window! Emir felt terrible. He knocked on the door and apologised to the old lady who lived there. To his surprise, she laughed and said, "Don\'t worry!" Then she gave him a cookie and a glass of lemonade.')],
'b1x-w1':[('Örnek 2 · farklı fikirlerle','Dear Mr Clark,\nThanks for letting us know about the new English club – I think it\'s a fantastic idea and I\'d definitely like to be part of it.\nTuesday would suit me better. On Fridays I usually visit my grandparents straight after school, so I wouldn\'t be able to come.\nAs for activities, I\'d love to do some drama, for example acting out short scenes in English. We could also have a book club where we read easy novels and talk about them.\nI\'d be happy to help. I could write a short article for the school website and talk to the students in my class about it.\nKind regards,\nEfe'),
 ('Örnek 3 · daha zengin dil (B2\'ye yakın)','Dear Mr Clark,\nThank you for your email. I think an English club is a brilliant idea, and I\'m sure lots of students will be interested.\nPersonally, Friday would work best for me. Not only do I have football practice on Tuesdays, but by Friday everyone is more relaxed and in the mood for something fun.\nIn terms of activities, it would be great to organise debates on topics that matter to teenagers, as they would help us practise speaking. Watching music videos and discussing the lyrics might also be popular.\nOf course I\'ll help spread the word. I could design a poster for the noticeboards and share it on our class group chat, which almost everyone reads.\nBest wishes,\nAslı')],
'b1x-w2':[('Örnek 2 · farklı fikirlerle','The day I met my baby sister\nI will never forget the fourteenth of March, three years ago. That morning, my dad woke me up early and told me that my mum was in hospital. I was so excited that I couldn\'t eat my breakfast!\nMy dad and I drove to the hospital together, and when we arrived, my mum was holding a tiny baby wrapped in a pink blanket. It was my sister, Ela.\nI\'ll always remember that day because my life changed completely. I wasn\'t an only child any more, and now Ela is my best friend – even when she takes my things without asking!'),
 ('Örnek 3 · daha zengin dil (B2\'ye yakın)','Lost in the mountains – and found\nThe day I will never forget began like any other school trip. Our class had gone hiking in the mountains, and my best friend and I stopped to take photos of a waterfall. By the time we looked up, the rest of the group had disappeared.\nAt first we laughed, but as the sky grew darker and our phones had no signal, we started to panic. Eventually, we decided to follow the river downhill, and after two long hours we finally saw the lights of a village.\nI will always remember that day, not because it was frightening, but because I discovered I could stay calm in a difficult situation – and because I have never been so happy to see my teacher!')],
'b1x-w3':[('Örnek 2 · farklı fikirlerle','After ten minutes on the bus, Maya realised she was going the wrong way. Instead of the city centre, the bus was heading out of town towards the airport.\nShe was on her way to a job interview at ten o\'clock, and it was already twenty past nine. She felt her heart beating faster. Then she noticed that the woman sitting next to her was wearing a uniform from the same company.\n"Excuse me," said Maya, "are you going to Green Office too?"\nThe woman smiled. "Yes, our new office is near the airport now." It turned out that Maya had been looking at the old address. She arrived early and got the job.'),
 ('Örnek 3 · daha zengin dil (B2\'ye yakın)','After ten minutes on the bus, Maya realised she was going the wrong way. The view outside the window, which should have been full of shops and offices, had turned into fields and farms.\nShe was supposed to be meeting her cousin, who was arriving from Germany, at the train station in half an hour. Panicking, she pressed the bell and jumped off at the next stop, only to find that the next bus back would not come for forty minutes.\nJust as she was about to give up, a farmer driving a small truck stopped and offered her a lift. Although she was nervous at first, he turned out to be very friendly and even told her funny stories about his cows.\nIn the end, Maya arrived at the station just as her cousin was walking out, and the two of them laughed about her adventure all the way home.')],
'b2x-w1':[('Örnek 2 · karşı görüşle','The question of whether students should wear uniforms is one that divides parents, teachers and students alike. In my opinion, schools should allow students to choose their own clothes.\n\nSupporters of uniforms often argue that they save families money. However, this is not always true. Uniforms frequently have to be bought from specific shops at high prices, and students still need ordinary clothes for evenings and weekends, so families end up paying twice.\n\nMore importantly, uniforms prevent young people from expressing their personality at an age when they are trying to discover who they are. Choosing what to wear helps teenagers develop confidence and make their own decisions.\n\nIt is also worth considering comfort. Many uniforms are made from cheap materials that are too hot in summer and too cold in winter, which can make it harder for students to concentrate.\n\nIn conclusion, although uniforms may appear to create equality, I believe their disadvantages are greater. A simple dress code that bans inappropriate clothing would be a fairer and more practical solution.'),
 ('Örnek 3 · daha zengin dil (C1\'e yakın)','School uniforms are a familiar sight in many countries, yet whether they actually benefit students remains a matter of considerable debate.\n\nFrom a financial point of view, the picture is mixed. Admittedly, uniforms remove the pressure to keep up with expensive fashion trends, which can be a significant burden for less well-off families. On the other hand, compulsory items sold by a single supplier can be surprisingly costly.\n\nThe argument that uniforms restrict self-expression is perhaps overstated. Students have countless other ways of showing their individuality, from their hairstyles and accessories to the music they listen to and the opinions they hold. Arguably, removing clothing as a source of competition allows them to focus on what they say rather than what they wear.\n\nA further point in favour of uniforms is safety. On school trips, for instance, teachers can identify their students instantly in a crowd.\n\nWeighing up these arguments, I am in favour of school uniforms, provided they are affordable and comfortable. Schools should consult students and parents when designing them, so that the uniform becomes a source of pride rather than resentment.')],
'b2x-w2':[('Örnek 2 · farklı bir uygulama','How a map app made me braver\n\nI have always had a terrible sense of direction. For years, I was too nervous to explore new places on my own in case I got lost. Then a map app changed everything.\n\nThe app shows you exactly where you are, gives you directions step by step and even tells you which bus to take and when it will arrive. It also suggests interesting places nearby, such as cafés, museums and parks, which I would never have discovered otherwise.\n\nThanks to it, I have become much more independent. Last summer I travelled alone to a city I had never visited, and I found my way around without any problems. My parents were amazed!\n\nWould I recommend it? Definitely, especially to anyone who feels anxious about travelling. However, I would also advise people to look up from the screen sometimes. Getting a little lost can be part of the adventure, and some of my best memories come from the moments when I ignored the app completely.'),
 ('Örnek 3 · daha zengin dil (C1\'e yakın)','The app that gave me back my evenings\n\nI used to be the kind of person who promised to start exercising "next Monday" – every Monday. That all changed when a friend persuaded me to download a simple habit-tracking app.\n\nAt first glance, it does very little. You choose the habits you want to build, such as drinking more water or going for a run, and tick them off each day. What makes it surprisingly powerful is the chain it creates: after a week of ticks, you become strangely reluctant to break it.\n\nWithin two months, I was running three times a week and reading before bed instead of scrolling through social media. The app did not make me more disciplined overnight, but it made my progress visible, and that turned out to be enormously motivating.\n\nI would wholeheartedly recommend it to anyone who struggles to stick to good intentions. One word of warning, though: choose one or two habits to start with. Try to change everything at once, and you may well end up changing nothing at all.')],
'b2x-w3':[('Örnek 2 · farklı bir yer','Burger Box: big flavours, small space\n\nIf you are looking for a quick, tasty meal in the city centre, Burger Box on Station Road is well worth a visit.\n\nThe restaurant is tiny, with just a few high tables and a long counter facing the open kitchen, so you can watch the chefs at work. The atmosphere is lively and the music is loud, which makes it popular with students but perhaps less suitable for a quiet conversation.\n\nThe menu focuses on burgers, and they are excellent. I tried the classic cheeseburger, which was juicy and served in a soft, freshly baked bun. The sweet potato fries were crispy and full of flavour. There are also two vegetarian options, which my friend said were just as good.\n\nThe service was fast and friendly, and our food arrived in less than ten minutes, even though the place was full.\n\nMy only complaint is the price of drinks, which seemed rather high compared with the food. Overall, though, I would definitely recommend Burger Box for a casual lunch with friends.'),
 ('Örnek 3 · daha zengin dil (C1\'e yakın)','A taste of the sea: Balıkçı Ahmet\n\nPerched at the end of a small fishing harbour, Balıkçı Ahmet looks, at first glance, like little more than a wooden hut. Do not be fooled. This modest family-run restaurant serves some of the freshest seafood I have ever eaten.\n\nThere is no printed menu. Instead, the owner, Ahmet, brings a tray of that morning\'s catch to your table and recommends how each fish should be cooked. We chose grilled sea bass, which arrived simply seasoned with lemon and olive oil, alongside a selection of home-made starters, including a remarkable smoked aubergine salad.\n\nThe service is warm rather than polished. Ahmet\'s teenage son, who was clearly still learning, forgot our drinks twice, but his enthusiasm more than made up for it. Prices are reasonable, though not cheap, which is hardly surprising given the quality of the ingredients.\n\nIf I had one reservation, it would be the lack of indoor seating, which makes the restaurant far less appealing on cold or windy evenings.\n\nNevertheless, for anyone who appreciates simple food cooked with care, Balıkçı Ahmet is an absolute must. Book ahead in summer, and ask for a table by the water.')],
'c1x-w1':[('Örnek 2 · farklı yöntemleri seçerek','City centres in many countries are facing a crisis. As shopping moves online and offices empty, once-busy streets are becoming lifeless, particularly after dark. Two possible remedies are creating more green spaces and encouraging people to live in the centre.\n\nGreen spaces have obvious appeal. Parks, tree-lined streets and small gardens make cities more attractive and healthier places to be, offering shade in summer and somewhere to relax during the working day. As one speaker in the discussion pointed out, parks give people a reason to spend time in a city rather than simply passing through it. However, parks alone cannot solve the problem of empty streets in the evening; once offices close, even the most beautiful square can feel deserted.\n\nEncouraging people to live in the centre addresses this issue more directly. Residents create a constant presence, supporting local shops, cafés and services at all hours, and their presence naturally makes streets feel safer. Many cities have successfully converted unused offices into flats, bringing new life to areas that had been empty for years. The main obstacle is cost, as central housing is often far more expensive than in the suburbs.\n\nIn my view, encouraging residential use is the more effective approach. Green spaces improve the quality of a city centre, but people bring it to life. Ideally, the two should go hand in hand: new housing projects should include parks and gardens, ensuring that city centres become not only busier but also more pleasant places to live.'),
 ('Örnek 3 · daha zengin dil (C2\'ye yakın)','The decline of the traditional city centre has been one of the more striking urban trends of recent decades. Among the remedies proposed, banning private cars and repopulating central areas with residents have attracted particular attention, and they merit careful comparison.\n\nA ban on private vehicles promises cleaner air, quieter streets and safer conditions for pedestrians and cyclists. Its critics, notably local retailers, fear that customers will simply take their custom elsewhere. This concern, while understandable, has not generally been borne out where such schemes have been implemented thoughtfully; on the contrary, pedestrianised streets often see an increase in footfall, as people linger rather than merely drive through.\n\nRepopulating city centres, by contrast, tackles the root of the problem: the absence of everyday life outside working hours. As one participant in the discussion observed, centres feel empty and unsafe at night precisely because nobody lives there. Converting redundant office blocks into homes would sustain local businesses, revive evening activity and reduce the need for long commutes. The difficulty lies in ensuring that such housing is affordable, rather than becoming the preserve of the wealthy.\n\nOn balance, I consider repopulation the more effective long-term strategy, since it creates the conditions in which a car-free centre could thrive. A street without cars but also without residents risks remaining just as lifeless as before. Policymakers would therefore be wise to prioritise housing, introducing traffic restrictions progressively as the resident population grows.')],
'c1x-w2':[('Örnek 2 · farklı önerilerle','Proposal: improving the college canteen\n\nIntroduction\nThis proposal identifies the main weaknesses of the college canteen and suggests how the available budget could best be used. It draws on comments from students in three different year groups.\n\nThe current situation\nAlthough the food itself is generally considered acceptable, the canteen suffers from several practical problems. There are no facilities for students who bring their own lunch, such as microwaves, and drinking water is only available if bought in plastic bottles. In addition, the canteen closes at 2 p.m., leaving students with afternoon classes nowhere to buy a snack.\n\nSuggestions\nI would recommend spending part of the budget on two or three microwaves and a free water fountain. These are relatively inexpensive but would make a significant difference to students\' daily lives. I would also suggest extending opening hours until 4 p.m., perhaps offering a smaller menu of sandwiches and fruit in the afternoon. Finally, introducing a monthly "student menu day", when dishes suggested by students are served, would encourage greater involvement.\n\nBenefits\nThese measures would save students money, reduce plastic waste and ensure that everyone has access to food throughout the day. Involving students in menu choices would also make the canteen feel more like a shared space.\n\nConclusion\nWith modest investment, the canteen could meet the needs of a far wider range of students. I hope the principal will consider these suggestions.'),
 ('Örnek 3 · daha zengin dil (C2\'ye yakın)','Proposal for a more sustainable and welcoming canteen\n\nPurpose\nThe aim of this proposal is to recommend improvements to the college canteen that would not only address students\' immediate concerns but also reflect the college\'s stated commitment to sustainability.\n\nKey issues\nFeedback gathered from the student council highlights three recurring complaints. Firstly, the menu relies heavily on processed food, with fresh options often sold out by midday. Secondly, the amount of single-use packaging is considerable, sitting uncomfortably with the college\'s environmental policy. Thirdly, the canteen is perceived as a place to eat quickly rather than somewhere students would choose to spend time.\n\nRecommendations\nA proportion of the budget could be allocated to forming a partnership with a nearby farm or market, ensuring a regular supply of seasonal produce at reasonable cost. Replacing disposable cups and containers with reusable alternatives, perhaps offered at a small deposit, would substantially reduce waste. Lastly, rearranging the seating to include a quieter area with plants and natural light would encourage students to linger and socialise.\n\nAnticipated benefits\nHealthier meals would support students\' concentration and well-being, while a visible reduction in waste would strengthen the college\'s credibility on environmental issues. A more inviting space, meanwhile, could foster a stronger sense of community across year groups.\n\nConclusion\nThese changes are achievable within the current budget and would bring benefits extending well beyond lunchtime. I would strongly encourage the principal to consider them.')],
'c1x-w3':[('Örnek 2 · farklı bir belgesel','The ocean in a single bottle\n\nI had always assumed that plastic pollution was a problem caused by careless people dropping litter on beaches. "Blue Silence", a documentary following a team of marine scientists across the Pacific, showed me how naive that view was.\n\nThe film traces the journey of plastic from our kitchens to the most remote parts of the ocean, where it breaks down into tiny particles that are eaten by fish and, eventually, by us. Its most memorable sequence takes place on an uninhabited island, where the beaches are covered in rubbish that has drifted thousands of kilometres.\n\nWhat changed my thinking was realising that recycling, which I had always considered the solution, is only a small part of the answer. Much of what we put in recycling bins is never actually recycled. The documentary convinced me that reducing the amount of plastic we use in the first place is far more important, and since watching it, I have changed many of my shopping habits.\n\nThe film is not perfect. At times the dramatic music feels manipulative, and some of the statistics are presented too quickly to absorb. Nonetheless, I would recommend "Blue Silence" to anyone who cares about the environment, and particularly to those who, like me, believe they are already doing enough. It is uncomfortable viewing, but that is precisely why it matters.'),
 ('Örnek 3 · daha zengin dil (C2\'ye yakın)','Rethinking old age: "The Long Afternoon"\n\nFew documentaries manage to overturn assumptions so gently, yet so thoroughly, as "The Long Afternoon", which follows five residents of a care home in northern Italy over the course of a year.\n\nI went into the film expecting a melancholy portrait of decline. What I found instead was a story of curiosity and reinvention. One resident, a former factory worker in her nineties, learns to paint for the first time; another, once a strict headmaster, forms an unlikely friendship with a teenage volunteer. The director never intrudes, allowing long, unhurried scenes to reveal character rather than imposing a narrative.\n\nThe film challenged my rather lazy assumption that old age is primarily a period of loss. It made me realise how often we speak about older people rather than listening to them, and how much our society loses as a result. I have since started visiting my own grandfather more regularly, not out of duty but out of genuine interest in his stories.\n\nIf the film has a flaw, it is that it largely avoids the harder realities of ageing, such as illness and loneliness, which some viewers may find evasive.\n\nEven so, I would recommend "The Long Afternoon" without hesitation, particularly to younger viewers. It is quietly moving, often funny and, above all, a powerful reminder that a life is never simply over.')],
}
